<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Uxmal</h1><p class="st-meta">700 CE – 1000 CE (approx.) · capital Uxmal</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The leading city of the Puuc hills of Yucatán, celebrated for the refined mosaic architecture of the Nunnery Quadrangle and the Governor's Palace.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Uxmal grew in the hilly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puuc">Puuc</a> region of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yucatan-peninsula">Yucatán</a>, where fertile soils supported farming despite the lack of surface water. Cisterns (chultunes) stored rainwater. The city became powerful in the 8th century and reached its height around 850-925.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its buildings show <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puuc">Puuc</a> style at its finest: smooth lower walls and upper façades of carved stone mosaic, with masks of the rain god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaac">Chaak</a>, latticework and serpents. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyramid-of-the-magician">Pyramid of the Magician</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nunnery-quadrangle">Nunnery Quadrangle</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governors-palace-uxmal">Governor's Palace</a> dominate the site. A ruler known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-chak">Lord Chak</a>, active around 900, appears to have built many of them. Causeways linked Uxmal with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabah">Kabah</a> and Nohpat, suggesting a regional state.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Uxmal declined after about 1000, probably as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chichen-itza">Chichen Itza</a> rose. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tutul-xiu">Xiu</a> lineage, later rulers of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mani">Maní</a>, claimed connections with it. It became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1996.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/uxmal.jpg" alt="Uxmal" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Sybz · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Uxmal_Pyramid_of_the_Magician.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>700 CE – 1000 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Uxmal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Lord Chak</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Maya religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Yucatec Maya</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Puuc kingdom of Uxmal</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=850&amp;focus=uxmal" aria-label="Uxmal on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2261 1424 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Uxmal lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2581 1680h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2581 1680h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=850&amp;focus=uxmal">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

